#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "4bbaf6b0-1057-5485-a3d8-2126e74fecdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3717" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ed796d54-235b-5ec0-b73f-5396d1ef99b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3718" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d47e7afd-bed0-5f2b-8635-ddd1a5303837" :question/math-academy-id "e-3719" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "92bba9e0-62db-5c96-8cbc-3cfbe8863a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-6738" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9834fc1-68ab-5c9f-98d1-9318078c44c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381220} {:answer/id #uuid "0133ec9f-af36-5f92-a87d-38a6cbff465b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381221} {:answer/id #uuid "8f371631-8e29-5248-b657-ca3cc7123192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381222} {:answer/id #uuid "f52e26a5-956a-5bea-893d-18bfc7eb0051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381223} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd23e03-9c78-58d1-b589-b5dd93ff6c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dd23e03-9c78-58d1-b589-b5dd93ff6c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381224} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcca2840-f4f8-5f50-b28d-ce12b2abd672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b24b1f9d-0768-54a7-a045-f1ebf26ee3d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125236" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/a7a36a62dcbbe0a0d67b9ccc8517eb7584e75d41d01227141229da3eed583500.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-3,4]$ and differentiable on $(-3,4).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-3,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-3$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-3)}{4-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-3)}{4+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-3,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{4}{7}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df0c4859-5dea-5999-8d14-38364fcd450d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314139} {:answer/id #uuid "a062fd1a-a751-5522-9439-7c2d18fd2251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314140} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7d6cd-76f1-5136-b989-3e280d92b241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314141} {:answer/id #uuid "75e29608-d101-5e74-a0f9-aeb93dacd868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314142} {:answer/id #uuid "19ceeb92-e248-5e16-81d4-f63546d4c29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7d6cd-76f1-5136-b989-3e280d92b241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314141} :answer-field/id #uuid "121e061c-8f59-5d38-a696-c7283ddd23ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70a9e682-5225-5573-9ac6-75c97731ce3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125237" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/74850221ee168c6911ee5b6c319d2128f67149f81790528940822e253905fc0f.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,3).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{3+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b611b5-e022-58ac-a112-ce62a70c2b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381227} {:answer/id #uuid "339c763a-b505-5cb6-94d0-384a3549bfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381228} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb8ec51-6a21-586a-abc8-f5f79feff775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381229} {:answer/id #uuid "0a82503b-99f4-5f08-b0d6-950d392a1c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381230} {:answer/id #uuid "7799dac2-a0a3-5e9f-b2fe-9bb31e63aafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7799dac2-a0a3-5e9f-b2fe-9bb31e63aafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381231} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d74fb0c-5981-5b86-a6a9-9ef17f6aad53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c2ace5b-4a86-5d8b-a101-1aeed1dde583" :question/math-academy-id "q-125251" :question/problem "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ on the interval $[4,16].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,16]$ and differentiable on $(4,16),$ hence, we can apply the Mean Value Theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,16)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=16$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(16)-f(4)}{16-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(16)-{log}_{2}⁡(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ c & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daa29262-3da5-53a0-a201-c2bfbbfde6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381234} {:answer/id #uuid "03aad5e8-ea9f-5fa6-a7cf-d1c1622aa435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381235} {:answer/id #uuid "e19f8e8e-d058-5637-96ec-fc4a145adffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381236} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcd3579-8d58-5d07-99d3-f04035f5b57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381237} {:answer/id #uuid "995151b3-0f0f-5b7f-b59f-f4eed6526c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bcd3579-8d58-5d07-99d3-f04035f5b57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381237} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d2304d-ce4d-54fe-ba1d-b0010c6f58e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a95efa0b-db7f-50aa-a72c-805b5062f119" :question/math-academy-id "q-125252" :question/problem "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡x$ on the interval $[9,81].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[9,81]$ and differentiable on $(9,81),$ hence, we can apply the Mean Value Theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (9,81)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=9$ and $b=81$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(81)-f(9)}{81-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(81)-{log}_{3}⁡(9)}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ c & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c61f722-e1db-5728-a3ab-366f444846c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381241} {:answer/id #uuid "c2feb9d2-7956-5587-a890-0f3d1de73f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381242} {:answer/id #uuid "b657eded-7b3e-5221-8420-0e617037df63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381243} {:answer/id #uuid "95fc7ad2-5d5b-544e-a288-831d6d1043ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381244} {:answer/id #uuid "62c55912-e6e5-5ad3-9589-654044b75fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2feb9d2-7956-5587-a890-0f3d1de73f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381242} :answer-field/id #uuid "82df94f4-f1e3-59e5-a3a6-adc4cb378e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ad1bad3-5f71-5c57-b099-896ea09a9f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-125259" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)={e}^{x}$\n- $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$\n- $h(x)=x|x|$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=x|x|$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb037793-c00e-51a0-b7e9-19ff2a8b5b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314160} {:answer/id #uuid "28f4edb0-b2e0-5f2d-b31b-e20351847375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/45db5f9f600f29e91fb40335f80fb28bb12f87fc4618c623354b91408f6bb09c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314161} {:answer/id #uuid "aee0cde4-4274-5c11-a1a6-35951fd3a6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/c4c45b3d88dd9821c1c5650e898d806b8887838c609548296040feae8915e40b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314162} {:answer/id #uuid "babd29ad-332f-5bf1-a097-5410fc085503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/09017ce160ebb2995e0c7c4efe81fd5444ba8cedd91dc270c69757b8b0eb1ae4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314163} {:answer/id #uuid "bf563876-8bda-570b-8deb-7aae372ff247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e50696609559e7996dda87b3566639e0f1772b0538b9b41e8311cb49b5c41a5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb037793-c00e-51a0-b7e9-19ff2a8b5b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314160} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e165b1-ef88-519e-80a6-6a1ee85a6ea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89cdcb08-8a88-5ba3-bb82-4bb65262eb4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125317" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b18e931-00c3-5105-9ffc-fde99764b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/c30de46f9f22348af7a80c808ee8374fee195f44d3333c66c10803b42866a8cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381248} {:answer/id #uuid "e15fc95e-f3f4-5585-b1ba-8bd6a58c8d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/5f439fc57fa9cccf4471379d0b5a4901cdc4fd0ccd997fe9ae2e52d7c0372b94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381249} {:answer/id #uuid "27424c7d-955b-50f9-be2d-6d0fc7550bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/83fffb6e46362e577cdc45bff6123555e070dc539505522ff134f2859954f9b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381250} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a0089f-a6a3-5e84-9576-a32315a63212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/2eba8ffb2094fab214cdebc0459ac6849c6bc2c37ac0f9bbd3fe026e44334800.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381251} {:answer/id #uuid "d6daffc5-fcf1-5c8a-b04a-e982c8883143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/ffab62f3c9f42cd73ea6de5c9363de2218ba21fc0f53651c056494b47efc323a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b18e931-00c3-5105-9ffc-fde99764b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/c30de46f9f22348af7a80c808ee8374fee195f44d3333c66c10803b42866a8cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381248} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fb4cef-55bd-5b8c-a091-6c6d42dabe4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225ac492-a1e7-5d42-8e19-52b35451f042" :question/math-academy-id "q-127009" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$  Hence, it is not continuous on $x=0.$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $p(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$  Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9b3820-2c88-588c-8149-74f4311067b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/d5927b5cc43ee1b5923b4d2aa4b4740e2bbc1c73b09b00525cb74767badc4808.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335678} {:answer/id #uuid "5193fae4-9ca9-5844-a705-8cd04c15abae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/787772f6d68af4df373cbfdd6b7a3cf3d313db7e03ecce36437f01068b738408.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335679} {:answer/id #uuid "233c301c-2915-5218-992c-ef218e3bd6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/f5d7744d62f94b96b8bd1600ca7f5fc752d1f83c8c8928e740261e808cb9e07b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335680} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c00683-baab-5743-97b8-4035232dabdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/8d37dc99a006ba89bc6a874d251a065fc714b260072740206f9cc3692b08c604.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335681} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9acf66-d993-5a0e-87cd-0752623c602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9acf66-d993-5a0e-87cd-0752623c602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3519f6d-17c0-5e34-805e-2f1cf9252804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cd65ad-908c-58d2-aca2-a54d10434503" :question/math-academy-id "q-127011" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c81cbbd4-4e7c-5e03-93f6-fab148bd5f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314167} {:answer/id #uuid "be200f29-1998-5d37-bf05-22e22786efd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314168} {:answer/id #uuid "99a27541-8b8e-5648-a51c-72fd6996b406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314169} {:answer/id #uuid "58bb6da6-8fe0-5437-a563-3216c42738e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314170} {:answer/id #uuid "028e6e8b-8793-5ec9-91d1-c87713cb4e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be200f29-1998-5d37-bf05-22e22786efd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314168} :answer-field/id #uuid "468bce35-a6af-512c-a602-d116db5280cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d78e600b-4326-5596-a935-0576240419b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127013" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29947c6a-4526-5c3b-b860-95d1678c90df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381255} {:answer/id #uuid "1679c9c9-d05c-5963-bee8-c993282c6f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381256} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bd7471-e64a-5857-9381-24b302e93a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381257} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9e14f1-eaa2-5dbd-886b-f1d35503555c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381258} {:answer/id #uuid "567b304d-e1c6-554e-a029-d61730bc21ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9bd7471-e64a-5857-9381-24b302e93a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381257} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac508cf5-09b0-5ef4-b8e5-58e91c27a935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8aa60ea3-4337-579e-bd3a-a287bb61ea84" :question/math-academy-id "q-2317" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{3}$ on the interval $[0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,1)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(1)-f(0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=3{c}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c\\in (0,1)$, we discard the negative value, therefore $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5416af2-c5a6-5183-8a75-14862a9fb852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5416af2-c5a6-5183-8a75-14862a9fb852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314195} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a11827e-ce9d-5354-a735-fdc0d1a904b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba9aff4f-f2e6-5d6a-bbda-2b487e4d8919" :question/math-academy-id "q-345063" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df5aaa6-3a50-569b-b90f-432bb3994961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df5aaa6-3a50-569b-b90f-432bb3994961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381262} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddda1bce-689e-5238-ba06-1b7f090e6042" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1627d046-23a7-5910-b67c-f60a8b23fe62" :question/math-academy-id "q-345316" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ on the interval $[-1,3].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,3)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=3$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-1)}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({3}^{2}+1)-((-1)^{2}+1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-2}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b969cfd-7cc1-55a7-9880-7b61b434f5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b629cd9686bb3f9b671a9412552ccd4161cb832d8e8426ee83a8955b81cc5831.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314198} {:answer/id #uuid "4a02ae06-70c4-57f4-a3e2-1626b85c36e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6cd605b5533bf0211bf75d3cc1d461fe41ee382e8799bd2337c4a53627eb0ca9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314199} {:answ