tinuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c246d93-1a7e-5ac0-bc7c-075112e3ff7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314212} {:answer/id #uuid "ff342493-7ac1-5c81-9e90-11e24667c2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314213} {:answer/id #uuid "350d3a49-76f7-5443-8488-eac6e90e4e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314214} {:answer/id #uuid "99185650-3aca-5788-80ed-0299e529036e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314215} {:answer/id #uuid "db8e3319-4087-53ea-ab8e-545a388b1106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db8e3319-4087-53ea-ab8e-545a388b1106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314216} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7271472-91ee-5dab-9fa6-a3d4527d95f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad37b0b8-9076-54d3-a5c8-dee0a591a349" :question/math-academy-id "q-48497" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ but has a corner at $x=1.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd7ab8f-f6de-565c-a223-7f34a8cb3f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381265} {:answer/id #uuid "8924ac61-7ef9-5ccf-88cd-f5737e08c48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381266} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d1c798-f3d6-5199-a817-97fa247c9aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381267} {:answer/id #uuid "df6f8197-8d78-5556-91de-67ebf90b894a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381268} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6b1924-b961-5957-9630-f67f9173903a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8924ac61-7ef9-5ccf-88cd-f5737e08c48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381266} :answer-field/id #uuid "cacf94eb-033d-5913-9cb7-53c138e6a58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c14a931-93db-572b-92db-617c82f326c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48500" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\n- $g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$\n- $h(x)=x-2$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ is not defined at $x=2$, so it is not continuous at $x=2$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,3].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x-2$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c18829-6760-54bb-b25f-d10c4378b1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062952} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c8907c-e186-5f42-8744-97afb6b8f647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062953} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1f2090-b915-5e6c-b1e5-56c49951daec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062954} {:answer/id #uuid "462aeff4-a2c8-50d9-a6f6-4392570ccc4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062955} {:answer/id #uuid "658bae93-2830-5aeb-a093-977c4122ea56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "658bae93-2830-5aeb-a093-977c4122ea56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(c) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062956} :answer-field/id #uuid "848fdf88-1fad-5e42-a7f9-34f9bc0e79ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb1393df-f078-5d0d-b6e1-3a860ee39d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48554" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/429c0a3eb7a6b1b0a99fba09eb7dd062f645669e99d39a919dc369467b4c373d.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-2,4]$ and differentiable on $(-2,4).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-2,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-2$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-2)}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-2,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{3}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc868210-90c8-5aef-8a96-299b3c7236db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314226} {:answer/id #uuid "dd764a30-26f8-5243-9774-99f981dcac04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314227} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e66a85-4cec-555e-926c-267fb5074e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314228} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a81f8-6c26-5bac-a646-1a6beefe08f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314229} {:answer/id #uuid "9c20212e-f576-5a79-9ba2-8ed2dbe1b52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd764a30-26f8-5243-9774-99f981dcac04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314227} :answer-field/id #uuid "52164d79-ea96-5cd6-a8b0-6bd7485d4f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37d9c80f-2cd5-546e-9463-84b02d5e64b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73349" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/6393810d90b8d340dbde041c43cb7e146cb59fedfcfd4a678827d291ea502b00.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,4]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,4).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-5)}{4-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-2}{4+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "807eff2f-33f9-5678-87ed-4ec523447a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314233} {:answer/id #uuid "16c1ff25-221b-595c-b770-a6c70a3c76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314234} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8443f2-f2bb-5db0-93f6-66a50a9920df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314235} {:answer/id #uuid "7aad2e0d-d3c8-552f-a49e-c35b2a0b0b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314236} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2d3ddb-93d5-5d4d-9b86-b078b3600fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aad2e0d-d3c8-552f-a49e-c35b2a0b0b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314236} :answer-field/id #uuid "0422f357-1fa3-5fa9-9b29-4197de680874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75e8bebf-29a4-5c2b-8823-7c997148cfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-73354" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/029a51dccba9f1ec927c820fb8adb72e926b88a881f4646090ac651ef29da271.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on $(-5,3).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{3+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1177f855-7da3-5adb-b1cf-7ae26d427d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381272} {:answer/id #uuid "13419a9a-43c1-5e94-a26d-003d8c5e5f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381273} {:answer/id #uuid "9a54d50b-2e7b-5ec2-b9a7-21389e6d5259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381274} {:answer/id #uuid "1693953c-1650-5185-8dee-15381ea843e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381275} {:answer/id #uuid "1e084d37-416d-5934-89e7-93568c2821f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1693953c-1650-5185-8dee-15381ea843e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381275} :answer-field/id #uuid "14836f09-3816-54d5-84c9-deca04cb032c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd7527af-21b3-58e5-acfe-e4b81f88473a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77864" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$ is equivalent to $p(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ so it is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca80d048-3584-53f5-8bb1-1f66a7c3c2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/db518cdc174cb245f2479f44a185266f274d9c6f2b5ef90f3d752aadf761726d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314247} {:answer/id #uuid "f4dff307-835e-50bb-a926-eb8ec8f9f146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/535745a110dcf5c361965127a8d25455687415c547f8ee7406d0add33121589b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314248} {:answer/id #uuid "d35f131b-276b-5124-9132-da7921b90833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ef3499bbe65662a44c92b3c5ce5023bfa74b8cbd2f37dc182bdc62dbcffba1f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314249} {:answer/id #uuid "2e684ecb-b0c3-5233-aa04-ae52638e236f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/49db2f4f9b88672a0d3a8229f80850b6187f2cebc59a8a334f869d3ffa7a56a6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314250} {:answer/id #uuid "5e09b422-07ff-5ae6-b667-d158ea9e29a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/266cc36afab26e5af24044f66b8689b3abc550409d40336375e896064b1badf3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca80d048-3584-53f5-8bb1-1f66a7c3c2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/db518cdc174cb245f2479f44a185266f274d9c6f2b5ef90f3d752aadf761726d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b85ffea3-7ffa-59c3-baf6-91fdff82d3bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58937f0c-40a0-5a69-a5bf-d0139202fa4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-77888" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}]}
